new_docs = {'1984-resilio-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-reconstitution-legitimacy-and-anchor-fragmentation-evaluation.md': "# Resilio remedy hardening attestation catastrophe recovery, reconstitution legitimacy, and anchor fragmentation evaluation\n\n## What current Resilio gets right\n\nCurrent official Resilio materials still deserve credit for being candid that `the state survives named regime shifts`, `we can recover from loss or corruption`, and `the recovered world is legitimately continuous with the prior governed world` are not one flat truth.\nThat candor is useful.\n\nThe strongest present ingredients are:\n\n- current `Cloning Sync` docs still say cloning any Sync instance by plain copy, disk cloner, or backup-image means is unsupported and may create two or more instances that do not transfer data to one another\n- current `Sync Storage folder` docs still say the storage folder keeps current configuration, auxiliary settings files, shares databases, dumps, and logs\n- current `Encrypted folders` docs still say recovery from an encrypted backup peer is possible only if RW and RO keys were saved and the encrypted folder was not removed from Sync so the database remains the same as initially created\n- the same current `Encrypted folders` docs still say local decryption requires the RW secret plus the path to the database file, which may have to be learned from debug logs\n- current `Service files missing / Cannot identify destination folder` docs still say deleting or corrupting `.sync` suspends synchronization, and the operator fix is to remove the share, delete `.sync`, and add it back, which creates a new synchronization instance\n- current `If your device is stolen` docs still say a serious recovery path requires backing up data, removing shares, unlinking the old identity if linked, deleting synced data from the storage folder, reinstalling Sync with settings removed, regenerating identity, relinking devices, and resharing folders\n- current `Updating installation to Resilio Sync v3` docs still say preserving configuration across update depends on keeping the same command-line parameters, user, and storage path, which reinforces how much recovery truth depends on retained anchors\n- older but still current `Resilio Sync change log` materials still record corrupt-database and lost-certificate fixes, reminding us that crash or corruption recovery is not a merely hypothetical seam\n\n## Where the current contract still fragments\n\nThe problem is not that Resilio lacks disaster-recovery clues.\nThe problem is that it still does not produce one first-class, case-scoped **catastrophe-recovery legitimacy** object.\n\nToday an operator can often infer only weaker truths such as:\n\n- the bytes were recovered, but the same governing identity, share instance, or database lineage may not have been preserved\n- the storage folder survived, but the operator still has to know which files are authoritative anchors and which merely happened to remain on disk\n- encrypted recovery is possible, but only under exact saved-key and unchanged-database preconditions\n- `.sync` corruption can be repaired, but the repair itself may create a new synchronization instance rather than reconstitute the prior one\n- theft response can sever the old world and establish a safer new one, but that is re-foundation, not same-world recovery\n- a copied image may look like a fast restore path, but the vendor explicitly says cloning is unsupported and may create twin runtimes with strange behavior\n\nThose are useful clues.\nThey are not the same as an explicit answer to `after loss, corruption, theft, or catastrophic world damage, what exactly was preserved, what was regenerated, what continuity sentence is still honest, and which stronger same-world recovery sentence must remain blocked?`\n\n## Why that matters for AnonSync\n\nAnonSync needs a stronger sentence than `the state survived upgrades and normal world transitions`.\nIt needs to support claims such as:\n\n- the prior governed world is lost, but a safe re-founded successor world has been established\n- recovery is possible only if named anchors survive, such as keys, database lineage, storage continuity, or explicit receipts\n- user-visible bytes were recovered, but governing continuity is blocked because the share instance or identity had to be regenerated\n- the named catastrophe class was recovered for the governed slice, but broader same-world reconstitution remains blocked\n\nSo AnonSync should not clone Resilio's present contract at this seam.\nIt should borrow the candor about unsupported cloning, storage-folder anchor dependence, encrypted-node recovery conditions, `.sync`-loss repair branching, and stolen-device reset flows, while replacing the fragmented operator story with one first-class family for **catastrophe recovery, reconstitution legitimacy, and anchor-bounded continuity truth**.\n", '1985-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-contract-sheet-page-catastrophe-class-anchor-retention-and-reconstitution-budget-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation catastrophe-recovery contract sheet page — catastrophe class, anchor retention, and reconstitution budget\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the compact contract for deciding whether a governed state that survived ordinary churn and named regime shifts can still be honestly recovered after catastrophic loss, corruption, theft, or control-world collapse.\nIt exists so the product can distinguish `catastrophe unreviewed`, `bytes salvage only`, `anchor-dependent recovery possible`, `reconstitution blocked`, `safe re-foundation only`, `named catastrophe recovered for named slice`, and `same-world recovery ceiling blocked`.\n\n## Core fields\n\n- catastrophe-recovery identifier\n- source regime-shift receipt identifier\n- governing durable sentence before catastrophe\n- named catastrophe class under review\n- governed slice identifier\n- preserved-bytes witness status\n- preserved-share-instance witness status\n- preserved-identity witness status\n- preserved-storage-root witness status\n- preserved-database-lineage witness status\n- preserved-secret-or-key anchor status\n- preserved-control-surface receipt status\n- `.sync` capsule integrity status\n- clone-or-image-restore exposure status\n- forced-reset or forced-regeneration status\n- safe successor-world identifier\n- reconstitution budget summary\n- current catastrophe-recovery class\n- highest currently safe catastrophe-recovery sentence\n- strongest blocked stronger sentence\n- next fact that upgrades catastrophe-recovery standing now\n- next fact that collapses catastrophe-recovery standing now\n\n## Catastrophe-recovery classes\n\nThe page must model at least these distinct classes:\n\n- catastrophe class unreviewed\n- bytes salvage only, governing continuity unknown\n- anchor-dependent recovery possible, witness missing\n- encrypted-backup or retained-database path conditionally recoverable\n- `.sync` or instance capsule lost, same-instance continuity blocked\n- clone-derived restoration unsupported\n- safe re-foundation required, prior world continuity severed\n- named catastrophe recovered for named slice only\n- same-world reconstitution proven for named slice only\n- broader all-catastrophe recovery robustness blocked\n\n## Minimum required comparisons\n\nThe page must force explicit comparison between:\n\n- ordinary migration and catastrophe recovery\n- preserved bytes and preserved governing anchors\n- same-world reconstitution and successor-world re-foundation\n- retained database lineage and regenerated share instance\n- retained identity and newly generated identity\n- supported recovery path and unsupported clone-like shortcut\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- `the files came back` must never silently upgrade to `the same governed world came back`.\n- `the storage folder exists` must never silently upgrade to `the right anchors survived inside it`.\n- `re-added and syncing again` must never silently upgrade to `same share instance recovered`.\n- `new identity is active` must never silently upgrade to `prior identity continuity preserved`.\n- `image restore booted` must never silently upgrade to `supported same-world recovery`.\n', '1986-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-review-page-can-the-governed-state-be-recovered-after-loss-corruption-theft-or-capsule-collapse-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation catastrophe-recovery review page — can the governed state be recovered after loss, corruption, theft, or capsule collapse?\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the operator-facing review that answers the practical catastrophe question after steady-state durability and named regime-shift robustness are already good enough: given that the target state used to be true, can it still be recovered after a destructive event without lying about whether the same governed world survived?\n\n## Primary review prompts\n\nThe review must answer these prompts in order:\n\n1. **Which durable or regime-shift receipt is the starting basis for this catastrophe review?**\n2. **What exact catastrophe is under review: corruption, storage loss, stolen device, `.sync` capsule loss, unsupported clone restore, key loss, or world reset?**\n3. **Which anchors survived: bytes, databases, keys, identity, `.sync` capsule, receipts, storage root, control records?**\n4. **Which parts must be regenerated, re-added, relinked, reshared, or re-founded?**\n5. **Does the proposed recovery preserve the same world, or does it create a successor world that only inherits some bytes or policy?**\n6. **What is the strongest catastrophe-recovery sentence the product may honestly publish now?**\n\n## Review sections\n\n### 1. Pre-catastrophe basis board\n\nShow:\n\n- source durable or regime-shift receipt\n- target sentence before catastrophe\n- governed slice\n- strongest honest sentence before failure\n\n### 2. Catastrophe-class board\n\nShow:\n\n- catastrophe class under review\n- whether the event destroyed bytes, anchors, identity, or only control capsule state\n- whether a supported recovery path exists\n- whether recovery depends on pre-saved anchors\n\n### 3. Anchor-retention board\n\nShow:\n\n- bytes retention\n- database-lineage retention\n- key or secret retention\n- identity retention\n- `.sync` capsule retention\n- storage-root retention\n- receipt and proof retention\n\n### 4. Re-foundation and regeneration board\n\nShow:\n\n- whether Sync instance must be re-added\n- whether identity must be regenerated\n- whether folders must be reshared or reconnected\n- whether the resulting world is same-world recovery or successor-world re-foundation\n\n### 5. Recovery sentence chooser\n\nThe review must output one and only one primary sentence class such as:\n\n- catastrophe unreviewed\n- bytes salvage only, continuity unknown\n- anchor-dependent recovery possible\n- encrypted-backup path conditional on retained keys and database lineage\n- `.sync` loss repaired by creating new synchronization instance\n- stolen-device response requires successor-world re-foundation\n- named catastrophe recovered for named slice only\n- same-world reconstitution proven for named slice only\n- broader all-catastrophe recovery robustness blocked\n\n## Hard rules\n\nThe review must never let an operator hide:\n\n- regenerated identity behind `recovered`\n- a new synchronization instance behind `same share restored`\n- restored bytes behind `full continuity`\n- unsupported clone restoration behind `rebuild succeeded`\n- missing anchor retention behind `probably recoverable`\n', '1987-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-proof-page-anchor-ledger-rebuild-path-and-reconstitution-ceiling-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation catastrophe-recovery proof page — anchor ledger, rebuild path, and reconstitution ceiling\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the evidence-heavy proof surface for determining whether a governed state can be recovered after catastrophic damage, and exactly how strong that recovery sentence may be.\nIt proves the ceiling on any claim that tries to say the system is not merely usable again, but legitimately continuous with the governed world that existed before the catastrophe.\n\n## Required evidence blocks\n\n### 1. Pre-catastrophe basis ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- source durable or regime-shift receipt\n- target sentence before catastrophe\n- governed slice\n- pre-catastrophe continuity class\n\n### 2. Catastrophe-class ledger\n\nFor the named catastrophe preserve:\n\n- catastrophe class\n- destructive scope\n- whether loss touched bytes, anchors, identity, control capsule, or storage world\n- whether the event itself already severs same-world continuity\n\n### 3. Anchor-retention ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- bytes retained or lost\n- storage folder retained or lost\n- database lineage retained or lost\n- keys or secrets retained or lost\n- identity retained or regenerated\n- `.sync` capsule retained, corrupted, or recreated\n- receipts, proofs, or rulebook anchors retained or lost\n\n### 4. Recovery-path ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- supported recovery path or unsupported shortcut\n- re-add, relink, reconnect, reshare, or decrypt steps required\n- exact anchors consumed by the recovery path\n- exact elements regenerated or replaced\n- whether the path yields same-world reconstitution or successor-world re-foundation\n\n### 5. Contradiction and downgrade ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- clone-like restore exposure\n- twin-instance exposure\n- regenerated-identity exposure\n- new synchronization-instance exposure\n- contradictory witnesses after recovery\n- resulting downgrade class\n\n### 6. Claim ceiling\n\nRender:\n\n- highest honest bytes-recovered sentence\n- highest honest governing-world continuity sentence\n- highest honest same-world reconstitution sentence\n- blocked stronger sentence and exact blocker\n\n## Evidence classes\n\nThe page must distinguish at least:\n\n- bytes recovered only\n- supported anchor-dependent path not yet executed\n- retained-database and retained-key conditional recovery only\n- same-instance continuity blocked\n- same-identity continuity blocked\n- successor-world re-foundation only\n- unsupported clone-derived recovery\n- named catastrophe recovered for named slice only\n- same-world reconstitution proven for named slice only\n- broader all-catastrophe robustness blocked\n\n## Hard rules\n\nThe proof page must never treat:\n\n- recovered files as proof of recovered governance\n- one surviving anchor as proof that all required anchors survived\n- a new working Sync instance as proof of same-instance continuity\n- regenerated trust material as proof of preserved trust continuity\n- unsupported clone restoration as proof of supported reconstitution\n', '1988-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-timeline-page-loss-corruption-reset-reseed-reconnect-and-reissue-events-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation catastrophe-recovery timeline page — loss, corruption, reset, reseed, reconnect, and reissue events\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the ordered timeline for understanding how a governed state moved through a catastrophic event and any later recovery path.\nIt exists so later operators can see whether the world was genuinely reconstituted, partially salvaged, or fully re-founded after the break.\n\n## Required event families\n\nThe timeline must be able to render at least these event families:\n\n- pre-catastrophe receipt issued\n- corruption or destructive-loss event detected\n- `.sync` capsule missing or corrupted\n- storage root lost or replaced\n- identity compromised or stolen-device response opened\n- backup or encrypted-node anchor located\n- key, database, or receipt anchor verified\n- re-add or re-share required\n- identity regenerated\n- successor world created\n- decrypt or reseed operation started\n- reconnect completed\n- same-world continuity disproved\n- named catastrophe recovery confirmed\n- reconstitution ceiling narrowed or upgraded\n- post-recovery recertification completed\n\n## Event cards\n\nEvery event card must preserve:\n\n- event identifier\n- event class\n- timestamp\n- affected governed slice\n- prior class\n- resulting class\n- anchor-retention effect\n- continuity effect\n- manual step required\n- resulting stronger sentence blocked or unlocked\n\n## Fixed rendering order\n\nEvery catastrophe-recovery timeline must render the same sections in the same order:\n\n1. **Pre-catastrophe basis**\n2. **Catastrophe and anchor-loss events**\n3. **Anchor discovery and recovery-path selection**\n4. **Regeneration, rebind, or re-foundation events**\n5. **Post-recovery recertification and final sentence**\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- The timeline must never hide a severed world behind a later calm surface.\n- The timeline must never hide regenerated identity behind `recovered`.\n- The timeline must never hide new synchronization instance creation behind `repaired`.\n- The timeline must never let a later successful reconnect erase the destructive break that happened in between.\n', '1989-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-lineage-receipt-page-anchor-scope-reconstitution-class-and-blocked-stronger-sentences-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation catastrophe-recovery lineage receipt page — anchor scope, reconstitution class, and blocked stronger sentences\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the durable receipt for the strongest honest catastrophe-recovery sentence after reviewing one or more destructive events and recovery paths.\nIt is the page a later operator opens when they need to know what was lost, what anchors survived, whether the same governed world was reconstituted or replaced, and which stronger sentence remained blocked.\n\n## Required receipt fields\n\nThe receipt must preserve:\n\n- catastrophe-recovery identifier\n- source durable or regime-shift receipt identifier\n- named governed slice\n- named catastrophe class\n- current catastrophe-recovery class\n- bytes retention status\n- database-lineage retention status\n- key or secret retention status\n- identity retention status\n- `.sync` capsule retention status\n- storage-root retention status\n- supported recovery-path status\n- forced-regeneration status\n- safe successor-world identifier if any\n- highest honest catastrophe-recovery sentence\n- strongest blocked stronger sentence\n- exact blocker summary\n- next evidence needed for upgrade\n- next evidence that would force downgrade\n- issuance timestamp\n- issuer identity\n\n## Canonical sentence families\n\nThe receipt must support at least these sentence families:\n\n- the target state was previously durable, but catastrophe recovery has not been reviewed\n- bytes were recovered for the named slice, but governing continuity remains unproven\n- the named catastrophe is recoverable only if the retained keys, database lineage, and other anchors remain available\n- the slice is usable again, but `.sync` or instance-capsule loss forced a new synchronization instance\n- the catastrophe was answered by safe successor-world re-foundation, not same-world recovery\n- the named catastrophe was recovered for the named slice only\n- same-world reconstitution is proven for the named slice only\n- broader all-catastrophe continuity or universal recoverability is blocked\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- The receipt must never collapse `usable again` into `same world restored`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `new instance works` into `old instance survived`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `keys existed somewhere` into `all anchor requirements were satisfied`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `reinstall and relink succeeded` into `continuity was preserved`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `clone did not visibly fail yet` into `supported recovery`.\n'}

for name, content in new_docs.items():
    (DOCS / name).write_text(content, encoding='utf-8')

readme_add = '\n\n## Revision addendum — catastrophe recovery, reconstitution legitimacy, and anchor-bounded continuity truth after rev0473\n\nThis tranche locks the next seam around **whether a state that survives ordinary churn and named regime shifts can still be honestly recovered after catastrophic loss, corruption, theft, or control-capsule collapse**.\nThe key decisions now made explicit in the archive are:\n\n- **regime-shift robustness is a first-class rung, but it is weaker than catastrophe-recovery legitimacy**\n- **bytes salvage only, anchor-dependent recovery possible, `.sync`-capsule loss with new-instance repair, safe successor-world re-foundation, named catastrophe recovered for named slice only, same-world reconstitution proven for named slice only, and broader all-catastrophe continuity blocked are different truths**\n- **surviving bytes are weaker than surviving anchors, surviving anchors are weaker than same-world reconstitution, and same-world reconstitution is weaker than broader all-catastrophe recoverability**\n- **re-added and syncing again, regenerated identity, copied-instance restore, and calm after reinstall can no longer silently impersonate same-world recovery**\n- **every serious catastrophe-recovery sentence now needs one receipt that preserves catastrophe class, anchor retention, forced regeneration, same-world versus successor-world outcome, highest honest recovery sentence, and the blocked stronger continuity sentence**\n\nNew docs added in this tranche:\n\n- `1984-resilio-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-reconstitution-legitimacy-and-anchor-fragmentation-evaluation.md`\n- `1985-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-contract-sheet-page-catastrophe-class-anchor-retention-and-reconstitution-budget-interface-spec.md`\n- `1986-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-review-page-can-the-governed-state-be-recovered-after-loss-corruption-theft-or-capsule-collapse-interface-spec.md`\n- `1987-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-proof-page-anchor-ledger-rebuild-path-and-reconstitution-ceiling-interface-spec.md`\n- `1988-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-timeline-page-loss-corruption-reset-reseed-reconnect-and-reissue-events-interface-spec.md`\n- `1989-remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery-lineage-receipt-page-anchor-scope-reconstitution-class-and-blocked-stronger-sentences-interface-spec.md`\n\nWhat this tranche contributes to the larger doctrine:\n\n- `we got the files back` can no longer hide whether the same governed world came back\n- retained bytes can no longer silently impersonate retained keys, databases, identity, or instance continuity\n- a safe successor world can no longer masquerade as same-world recovery\n- later operators can open one receipt and see exactly which anchors survived, which had to be regenerated, whether the old world was reconstituted or replaced, and which stronger continuity sentence the product refused to make\n'
status_add = '\n\n## Revision addendum — catastrophe recovery, reconstitution legitimacy, and anchor-bounded continuity truth after rev0473\n\nThe next seam after **regime-shift robustness across named world transitions** is now explicit:\n\n- the archive can already say whether a target state survives ordinary churn and whether it survives upgrades, service-account changes, storage relocation, config-mode flips, and other named regime shifts strongly enough to keep the same governing sentence\n- it still needed to own the harder truth of **whether that state can be recovered after destructive loss, corruption, theft, `.sync` capsule collapse, or other catastrophe without silently lying about which anchors survived and whether the same governed world still exists**\n- current Resilio docs reinforce that gap because cloning remains explicitly unsupported, storage keeps configuration and shares databases, encrypted-backup recovery depends on saved keys and unchanged database lineage, `.sync` loss repair can create a new synchronization instance, and stolen-device response explicitly severs the previous instance before establishing a new one\n\nThis pass turns that gap into a first-class product object: **the remedy-hardening-attestation catastrophe-recovery, reconstitution legitimacy, and anchor-bounded continuity case**.\n\nWhat is newly true in the archive:\n\n- **regime-shift robustness is weaker than catastrophe-recovery legitimacy**\n- **bytes salvage only, anchor-dependent recovery possible, `.sync` capsule loss with new-instance repair, clone-derived recovery unsupported, safe successor-world re-foundation, named catastrophe recovered for named slice only, and same-world reconstitution proven for named slice only are separate public truths**\n- **surviving bytes are weaker than surviving anchors, surviving anchors are weaker than same-world reconstitution, and same-world reconstitution is weaker than broader all-catastrophe recoverability**\n- **re-added and syncing again, regenerated identity, copied-instance restore, and calm after reinstall now degrade into evidence inputs instead of impersonating same-world recovery**\n- **every serious catastrophe-recovery sentence now needs one receipt that preserves catastrophe class, anchor retention, forced regeneration, same-world versus successor-world outcome, highest honest recovery sentence, and the blocked stronger continuity sentence**\n\nWhat remains intentionally true:\n\n- durability across ordinary churn still matters\n- named regime-shift robustness still matters\n- world-continuity discipline still matters\n- but none of those may substitute for one explicit answer about what survived the catastrophe, what had to be regenerated, and whether the old world was truly reconstituted or only replaced with a safer successor\n'
eval_add = '\n\n## Revision addendum after rev0473 — why catastrophe recovery, reconstitution legitimacy, and anchor-bounded continuity now sit on the non-clone side\n\nCurrent official Resilio docs are still admirably candid that `the state was durable`, `the state survived named regime shifts`, `we can still get the files back`, and `the same governed world was legitimately reconstituted` are not one flat truth.\n`Cloning Sync` still says cloning any Sync instance is unsupported and may create multiple instances that do not transfer data to one another.\n`Sync Storage folder` still says storage keeps current configuration, auxiliary settings files, shares databases, dumps, and logs.\n`Encrypted folders` still says recovery from an encrypted backup peer is possible only if RW and RO keys were saved and the encrypted folder was not removed from Sync so the database remains the same as initially created, and that local decryption requires the RW secret plus the path to the database file.\n`Service files missing / Cannot identify destination folder` still says deleting or corrupting `.sync` suspends synchronization and that the operator fix is to remove the share, delete `.sync`, and add the share back, which creates a new synchronization instance.\n`If your device is stolen` still says the serious response path is to back up data, remove shares, unlink identity if linked, remove synced data from storage, reinstall with settings removed, regenerate identity, relink devices, and reshare folders, explicitly breaking connection with the stolen device.\n`Updating installation to Resilio Sync v3` still says preserving configuration across update depends on keeping the same parameters, user, and storage path, reinforcing how much any later recovery truth depends on retained anchors.\nOlder but still current `Resilio Sync change log` materials still record corrupt-database and lost-certificate fixes, reminding us that corruption and rebuild are first-class seams rather than edge cases.\n\nThis is strong operational candor.\nIt is also exactly why AnonSync should not clone the present contract.\nOne ordinary answer to `after catastrophic loss or compromise, what exactly did we recover, what anchors survived, did we reconstitute the same world or create a safer successor world, and which stronger continuity sentence must remain blocked?` still depends on combining anti-cloning guidance, storage-folder anatomy, encrypted-node preconditions, `.sync`-loss repair branching, stolen-device reset flow, upgrade-path anchor discipline, and remembered support folklore.\n\nSo this tranche freezes a stronger replacement line: **bytes salvage only, anchor-dependent recovery possible, new-instance repair, safe successor-world re-foundation, named catastrophe recovered, and same-world reconstitution become separate modeled truths.**\n\nThat is why this revision adds five more first-class pages: **Remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery contract sheet**, **catastrophe-recovery review**, **catastrophe-recovery proof**, **catastrophe-recovery timeline**, and **catastrophe-recovery lineage receipt**.\n'
sources_add = "\n\n## rev0474 source set — catastrophe recovery, reconstitution legitimacy, and anchor-bounded continuity\n\nThe most load-bearing source set for this pass was:\n\n- Resilio's current `Cloning Sync` article, which still says cloning any Sync instance by plain copy, hard-drive cloner, or backup-image means is unsupported and may create multiple instances that do not transfer data to one another.\n- Resilio's current `Sync Storage folder` article, which still says the storage folder keeps current configuration, auxiliary settings files, shares databases, dumps, and logs.\n- Resilio's current `Encrypted folders` article, which still says recovery from an encrypted backup peer requires saved RW and RO keys and requires not removing the encrypted folder so the database remains the same as initially created, and that local decryption requires the RW secret plus the path to the database file.\n- Resilio's current `Service files missing / Cannot identify destination folder` article, which still says deleting or corrupting `.sync` suspends synchronization and that the repair path is to remove the share, delete `.sync`, and add it back, creating a new synchronization instance.\n- Resilio's current `If your device is stolen` article, which still says the response path is to back up data, remove shares, unlink identity if linked, remove synced data from storage, reinstall with settings removed, regenerate identity, relink devices, and reshare folders, explicitly breaking connection with the stolen device.\n- Resilio's current `Updating installation to Resilio Sync v3` article, which still says preserving configuration across update depends on keeping the same command-line parameters, user, and storage path.\n- Resilio's older but still current `Resilio Sync change log`, which still records corrupt-database and lost-certificate fixes that reinforce catastrophe recovery as a live seam.\n\nThose sources were enough to tighten the line again:\n\n- current Resilio still deserves credit for candid recovery ingredients\n- but current Resilio still answers `after catastrophe, what was actually recovered and how much governing continuity remains honest?` too diffusely\n- AnonSync should therefore prefer explicit remedy-hardening-attestation-catastrophe-recovery sheets, reviews, proofs, timelines, and lineage receipts over overloaded storage-folder lore, encrypted-node caveats, `.sync` repair advice, stolen-device reset steps, and anti-cloning warnings\n\nPrimary sources:\n\n- Cloning Sync\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/360018421940-Cloning-Sync\n\n- Sync Storage folder\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/206664690-Sync-Storage-folder\n\n- Encrypted folders\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/207370466-Encrypted-folders\n\n- Service files missing / Cannot identify destination folder\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/205450225-Service-files-missing-Cannot-identify-destination-folder\n\n- If your device is stolen\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/204644049-If-your-device-is-stolen\n\n- Updating installation to Resilio Sync v3\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/31194252837779-Updating-installation-to-Resilio-Sync-v3\n\n- Resilio Sync change log\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/206216855-Resilio-Sync-change-log\n"

(ROOT / 'README.md').write_text((ROOT / 'README.md').read_text(encoding='utf-8') + readme_add, encoding='utf-8')
